{-
   `copyMemory` - the non-overlapping block copy, now that it is a ladder in the language rather
   than one instruction.

   It used to be `rep movsb` and nothing else, so there was nothing here to test: the instruction
   either copies `n` bytes or the machine is broken. What it is now is `moveMemory`'s ladder with the
   direction question taken out - the same rungs, the same overlapping ragged end, the same
   `blockCopy` above `width * 48` - and every one of those has an edge a length can sit on.

   So this is `MoveMemory.yana`'s sweep with the argument that file cannot make. There, what had to
   be proved was that a byte is read before the write that could reach it; here the regions never
   meet, and what has to be proved is the other half:

   - **every byte of the destination is written**, which the run check says;
   - **and nothing else is**, which is what the two guard bands either side of the destination are
     for. A store that ran wide - a ragged transfer placed at `n - W` is only correct because it
     *stops* at `n` - shows up nowhere in the copied region itself.

   The lengths are chosen against the rungs rather than by hand: the dense sweep walks every length
   to 200, which crosses 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128 several times over, and the coarse one walks 200 to
   1800 in steps of thirty-seven so that it lands either side of `width * 48` - 768 bytes at v2 and
   1536 at v3, which is the one boundary where the answer stops being this ladder's and becomes the
   machine's.

   **Both offsets are varied and not just the length.** The wide steps are unaligned transfers, and
   which bytes a step straddles is a function of the *address* and not only of the count - so a
   sweep at offset zero on both sides would run every rung with the source and the destination
   congruent, which is the one case where a bug in the ragged end can cancel out.
-}

import Test

-- The buffer, in two halves: the source below `apart` and the destination above it, far enough
-- apart that no length here can bring them within a register of each other. `copyMemory` promises
-- nothing about overlapping regions, so a test that let them touch would be testing a contract that
-- does not exist.
let apart = 4096 :: Size
let guard = 64 :: Size

-- `0, 1, 2, ...` from `first`, so that any byte's correct value is its own index and a clobber shows
-- up as a repeat. It wraps at 256, which is why every comparison below truncates too.
fn copyMemoryFill(buffer: %U8, at: Size, first: Size, count: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        store(buffer + at + i, truncate(first + i) :: U8)
        i = i + 1

-- One value everywhere, which is what the destination and its guard bands are painted with before
-- each copy: a byte the copy leaves behind still reads as this one.
fn paint(buffer: %U8, at: Size, value: Size, count: Size) -> {}:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        store(buffer + at + i, truncate(value) :: U8)
        i = i + 1

fn copyMemoryRuns(buffer: %U8, at: Size, first: Size, count: Size) -> Bool:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        if (*(buffer + at + i)) != (truncate(first + i) :: U8) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

fn same(buffer: %U8, at: Size, value: Size, count: Size) -> Bool:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < count:
        if (*(buffer + at + i)) != (truncate(value) :: U8) then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

{-
   One copy, checked at all three edges.

   The source starts at `from` and holds the ascending run; the destination starts at
   `apart + to` with `guard` painted bytes in front of it and `guard` behind. What has to hold
   afterwards is that the run arrived, and that both bands are exactly as they were.
-}
fn mismatches(buffer: %U8, from: Size, to: Size, count: Size) -> Int:
    let source = from
    let target = apart + to

    copyMemoryFill(buffer, source, 7, count)
    paint(buffer, target - guard, 219, guard + count + guard)

    copyMemory(buffer + target, buffer + source, count)

    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    if !copyMemoryRuns(buffer, target, 7, count) then bad = bad + 1
    if !same(buffer, target - guard, 219, guard) then bad = bad + 1
    if !same(buffer, target + count, 219, guard) then bad = bad + 1

    return bad

{-
   The named cases, one at a time so that a failure says which shape broke.

   Zero and one are the two ends of the ladder that are not rungs: a count at or below zero falls off
   the bottom of it and has to leave the destination alone, and a count of one is the single store.
-}
@test fn edges() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(8192)

    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 0), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 1), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 2), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 7), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 8), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 15), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 16), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 17), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 31), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 32), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 33), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 63), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 64), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 65), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 128), 0)
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 129), 0)

    -- A negative count, which `Array` and `String` can both arrive at from a subtraction and which
    -- the ladder answers by doing nothing at all rather than by testing for it.
    checkEqual(mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, 0 - 8), 0)

    freeHeap(buffer)

{-
   And the sweep: every length to 200 against nine pairs of offsets, which is where the rungs and
   the ragged end live.

   The offsets are not multiples of each other. Nine pairs from three values apiece, so the source
   and destination are congruent in three of them and skewed in six - and the skew is what makes an
   unaligned wide step straddle a line the aligned one does not.
-}
@test fn copyMemorySweep() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(8192)
    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &count = 0 :: Size

    while count <= 200:
        let &i = 0 :: Size
        while i < 3:
            let &j = 0 :: Size
            while j < 3:
                -- 0, 1 and 5: aligned, one byte off, and off by an odd amount larger than a word.
                let from = if i == 0 then 0 else if i == 1 then 1 else 5
                let to = if j == 0 then 0 else if j == 1 then 1 else 5

                bad = bad + mismatches(buffer, from, to, count)
                j = j + 1

            i = i + 1

        count = count + 1

    freeHeap(buffer)
    checkEqual(bad, 0)

{-
   And past the sweep, to the length where the ladder stops being the answer.

   `width * 48` is 768 bytes at v2 and 1536 at v3, and the step of thirty-seven is what lands either
   side of both without walking sixteen hundred lengths one at a time. Below it this is the vector
   loop with its four-register group; above it the copy is handed to `blockCopy`, which is the
   machine's own block operation and has to answer exactly the same thing.
-}
@test fn long() -> {}:
    let buffer = allocateHeap(8192)
    let &bad = 0 :: Int
    let &count = 200 :: Size

    while count <= 1800:
        bad = bad + mismatches(buffer, 0, 0, count)
        bad = bad + mismatches(buffer, 1, 0, count)
        bad = bad + mismatches(buffer, 0, 3, count)
        bad = bad + mismatches(buffer, 5, 11, count)
        count = count + 37

    freeHeap(buffer)
    checkEqual(bad, 0)
